<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The eleventh cohomology group of <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>
S.C. was partially supported by NSF RTG grant DMS-1502651.
This research was partially conducted during the period H.L served as a Clay Research Fellow. S.P. was supported in part by NSF grants DMS-2001502 and DMS-2053261</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Samir Canning
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hannah Larson
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sam Payne
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that the rational cohomology group <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes unless <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>. We show furthermore that <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate for all even <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> and deduce that <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is surprisingly well approximated by a polynomial in <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, we use <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> and its image under Gysin push-forward for tautological maps to produce many new examples of moduli spaces of stable curves with nonvanishing odd cohomology and non-tautological algebraic cycle classes in Chow cohomology.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Langlands program makes a number of striking predictions about the Hodge structures and Galois representations that appear in the cohomology of moduli spaces of stable curves; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Section 1.2]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. While the conjectured correspondence with algebraic cuspidal automorphic representations of conductor 1 remains out of reach, these representations have been classified up to weight 22 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, and some of the resulting predictions can now be verified unconditionally. Bergström, Faber and the third author recently proved that <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for all odd <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
For <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math>, the conjectural correspondence predicts that <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a direct sum of copies of <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> and hence should vanish in all cases where <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational.
We confirm this prediction unconditionally and show that <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes in an even wider range of cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology group <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero if and only if <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a direct sum of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n-1}{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n-1}{10}</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>; in particular, it decomposes as <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\oplus H^{0,11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\oplus H^{0,11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the pullbacks of the distinguished generator of <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to the weight 12 cusp form <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, under the <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>11</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{11}</annotation></semantics></math> forgetful maps, and describe the relations among these generators. In this way, we show that <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation isomorphic to the Specht module <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-10,1^{10}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-10,1^{10}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we address the Hodge structures and Galois representations that appear in other low degrees. The Langlands program predicts that the cohomology of <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> should be pure Hodge–Tate in all even degrees less than or equal to 20. This prediction was previously confirmed only in the cases where these cohomology groups are known to be generated by tautological classes, e.g. for <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>,
for <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, and for <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Our second result extends the confirmation of this prediction to a much wider range of cases. The proof is a double induction on <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The base cases are given by recent results of the first two authors, who showed that <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any even <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomology group <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It remains an open problem whether <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by algebraic cycle classes for even <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\leq k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\leq k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As an application of these two theorems, we show that the point count <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is surprisingly well-approximated by a polynomial in <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\sum_{i=0}^{6}h^{2i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})q^{d-i}+O(q^{d-13/2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>6</mn></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\sum_{i=0}^{6}h^{2i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})q^{d-i}+O(q^{d-13/2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The point count <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, as is <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> was determined by Getzler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>; it has an approximation to order <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(q^{n-13/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(q^{n-13/2})</annotation></semantics></math> by a polynomial in <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> minus the correction term <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n-1}{10}\tau(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n-1}{10}\tau(q)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(q)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the coefficient of <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in the Fourier expansion of the weight <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> cusp form <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike the cohomology groups in smaller odd degrees, <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonvanishing in a wide range of cases, including for large <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, as are all higher degree odd cohomology groups. Indeed, Pikaart showed that <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonvanishing for <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large, as is <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Similar nonvanishing statements in higher degrees follow immediately, by Hard Lefschetz. The bounds on <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> that come from Pikaart’s method are large, typically in the thousands. For instance, van Zelm computes that Pikaart’s method yields <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8069" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8069</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8069</annotation></semantics></math>. The bounds for <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,10})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> are not explicitly stated in the literature, but there is substantial evidence that such bounds should be far from optimal. While Pikaart’s constructions prove the existence of non-tautological algebraic cycle classes on <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 16192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>16192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 16192</annotation></semantics></math>, van Zelm proved that this holds for <math id="S1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Also, Bergström and Faber have recently shown that <math id="S1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>. They also prove that the nonvanishing of <math id="S1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math> follows from conjectural parts of the Langlands correspondence. Here, we prove the latter statement unconditionally, extend it to all higher genera, and also improve Pikaart’s bound for the nonvanishing of <math id="S1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\dots,g_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\dots,g_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct positive integers, and set <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1+g_{1}+\cdots+g_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1+g_{1}+\cdots+g_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11+2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0\mbox{ \ for }n\geq 11-k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11+2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0\mbox{ \ for }n\geq 11-k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 67</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As a further application, we prove the existence of non-tautological classes in the Chow rings <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> in a number of cases where this was not previously known.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> as a stack over <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> as in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>, the quotient <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})/R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})/R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is uncountable, as is the subgroup of <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by cycles algebraically equivalent to zero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This provides many new examples of <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is not tautological. In particular, the existence of non-tautological Chow classes is new for <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\leq n\leq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\leq n\leq 16</annotation></semantics></math>; for <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5,n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\leq n\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\leq n\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math>; for <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(6,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(6,n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\leq n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\leq n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>; and for <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,8)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,8)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,9)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Existence results for non-tautological classes come in two flavors. There are cases where one can write down <em id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explicit</em> examples of non-tautological Chow classes. Graber and Pandharipande gave the first such example when <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)=(2,20)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)=(2,20)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Van Zelm generalized their example to show the existence of explicit non-tautological Chow classes on <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 24</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. A nice feature of these examples is that they are non-tautological in both Chow and cohomology. There are also the <em id="S1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inexplicit</em> non-tautological Chow classes, which arise from the existence of odd cohomology. The first such examples are for <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1,n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1,n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, where the existence of a holomorphic 11-form implies that <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite dimensional <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Bergström and Faber showed that there is odd cohomology on <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, implying that there are non-tautological Chow classes as well, by results of Kimura and Totaro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>; see Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1 (Kimura [], Totaro []). ‣ 7. Application to Chow rings ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>. The examples provided by Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> are also of this inexplicit form. We do not know whether all of the non-tautological classes in these inexplicit cases are homologically equivalent to zero.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Methods</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Arbarello and Cornalba introduced an inductive method for studying cohomology groups of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and applied this to prove the vanishing of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. The same method was used to prove vanishing for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{7,9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{7,9\}</annotation></semantics></math> after establishing the additional base cases needed to run the induction, via point counting over finite fields <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Our proof of Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> start from the observation that the same induction can be used to control the Hodge structures and Galois representations that appear in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> even when these groups do not vanish. The first two authors recently established the base cases needed for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Running this induction when <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is even leads directly to Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.Doing so for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math> shows that <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> injects into a direct sum of copies of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> (Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.3. The case of degree 
          
            
              =
              k
              11 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>). This is enough to confirm the prediction that <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes whenever <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational, but a different argument is needed to prove that it vanishes whenever <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Arbarello–Cornalba induction uses the excision sequence for the pair of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with its boundary <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}:=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}:=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, along with the map</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">given by pullback to the normalization of the boundary. Our proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> uses the observation that (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1. Methods ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) is the first arrow in a natural chain complex whose <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th term may be identified with the cohomology of the normalization of the closure of the codimension <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> boundary strata with coefficients in a natural local system, the determinant of the permutation representation on the branches of the boundary divisor. This complex has several natural interpretations: it is the <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th weight-graded piece of the Feynman transform of the modular operad that takes the value <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. It is also the weight <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> row in the <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of a natural spectral sequence obtained via Poincaré duality from Deligne’s weight spectral sequence for the pair <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and has a natural interpretation as a decorated graph complex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, Section 2.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math>, we examine the first two maps in this complex. Assuming that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for all <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, a double induction on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> shows that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes whenever <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. In Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we prove the needed base cases, i.e. the vanishing of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, by explicit calculations using the generators and relations for <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. It should also be possible to deduce these base cases from results of Petersen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>; see Remark <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Remark 4.3. ‣ 4.3.3. Graphs with two genus 
            
              1
            
           vertices ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Structure of the paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we recall how to extract <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> with its Hodge structure or Galois representation from the work of Getzler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. We then describe generators and relations for this group and describe the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action and the pullback under tautological morphisms in terms of these generators. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we recall the inductive method of Arbarello and Cornalba and use it to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we present the inductive argument for vanishing of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, using the weight spectral sequence, assuming the vanishing for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>. We then prove the vanishing in the necessary base cases, for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, using the explicit generators and relations for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In Sections <a href="#S5" title="5. Application to point counting ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#S6" title="6. Higher odd cohomology groups ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and <a href="#S7" title="7. Application to Chow rings ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we prove Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Corollary 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>, and Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Notations and conventions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> either the <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge structure <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{\mathbb{C}},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{\mathbb{C}},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> or the absolute Galois representation <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{\text{\'{e}t}}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mtext>ét</mtext><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{\text{\'{e}t}}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Tate motive.
We say that <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pure Hodge–Tate</em> if it is isomorphic to a direct sum of the Betti or <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic realizations of powers of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> for the motive associated to the weight <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> modular form <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, whose Betti and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic realizations are <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> the Chow ring with rational coefficients of a variety or Deligne–Mumford stack <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgments</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are grateful to Jonas Bergström, Carel Faber, Dan Petersen, and Burt Totaro for helpful conversations. We especially thank Burt Totaro for suggesting an improvement of an earlier verison of Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1 (Kimura [], Totaro []). ‣ 7. Application to Chow rings ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, and thus Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>, and Dan Petersen for suggesting the proof sketch in Remark <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Remark 2.6. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>. We thank the referee for helpful comments on an earlier version of this article.</p>
