</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Genus <math id="S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we give explicit generators and relations for <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and describe the <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action and the pullback to boundary divisors in terms of these generators. These formulas will be used in Section <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS2" title="4.3.2. Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.2</span></a>, in our proof that <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Dimension and Hodge structure</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by explaining how to extract <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> with its Hodge structure or Galois representation from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
Getzler gives generating functions
that determine
the cohomology groups <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> with their <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-actions.
These formulas simplify substantially when forgetting the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action, so we begin by using Getzler’s formula to extract <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> non-equivariantly.
Below, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Tate motive, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Hodge structure associated to the space of cusp forms of weight <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+2</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, p. 489]</cite> for definition). We note that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology group <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n-1\choose 10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n-1\choose 10}</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖾</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Euler characteristic of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Grothendieck ring of equivariant mixed Hodge structures.
Getzler defines two families of generating functions <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{i}=\sum\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\mathcal{M}_{i,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖾</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{i}=\sum\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\mathcal{M}_{i,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{i}=\sum\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{i,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖾</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{i}=\sum\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{i,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> these generating functions are power series in the Hodge structures
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}</annotation></semantics></math> whose coefficients are symmetric functions.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">To get to the ordinary Euler characteristic generating function <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{e}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{e}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖾</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply
Getzler’s <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>rk</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rk}</annotation></semantics></math> functor, which is defined by setting the power sums to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. It sends <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{i,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖾</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{e}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{i,n})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{i,n})\frac{x^{n}}{n!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{i,n})\frac{x^{n}}{n!}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, p. 484]</cite>. We shall write <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=\mathrm{rk}(\mathbf{a}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=\mathrm{rk}(\mathbf{a}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}=\mathrm{rk}(\mathbf{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}=\mathrm{rk}(\mathbf{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are power series in <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}=\mathrm{rk}(\mathbf{b}_{0})=\sum_{n\geq 3}\mathsf{e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})\frac{x^{n}}{n!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}=\mathrm{rk}(\mathbf{b}_{0})=\sum_{n\geq 3}\mathsf{e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})\frac{x^{n}}{n!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Important for us is that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+x+b_{0}^{\prime}=e^{x}+\text{terms divisible by $\mathsf{L}$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>terms divisible by </mtext><mi>𝖫</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+x+b_{0}^{\prime}=e^{x}+\text{terms divisible by $\mathsf{L}$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we are interested in the coefficient of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{x^{n}}{n!}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{x^{n}}{n!}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equal to the negative of the multiplicity of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> by construction.
Applying <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>rk</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rk}</annotation></semantics></math> to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Theorem 2.5]</cite> relates <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the symbol <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Theorem 2.5]</cite> denotes plethysm of symmetric functions; applying <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>rk</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rk}</annotation></semantics></math> turns this plethysm into composition of functions, as can be seen from the properties characterizing plethysm in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Section 5.2]</cite>. In particular, we find</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}=a_{1}(x+b_{0}^{\prime})+\text{terms built from $a_{0}$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>terms built from </mtext><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}=a_{1}(x+b_{0}^{\prime})+\text{terms built from $a_{0}$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">The terms built from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot contribute to the coefficient of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. (We note that there is a small error in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Theorem 2.5]</cite>, which is corrected in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, p. 306]</cite>, but it occurs in these terms built from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus will not affect the outcome of our calculation.)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">To expand the right-hand side of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.1. Dimension and Hodge structure ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), apply <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>rk</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rk}</annotation></semantics></math> to the equation for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, p. 489]</cite>, and then plug in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+b_{0}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+b_{0}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (so substitute <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=x+b_{0}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=x+b_{0}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}(x+b_{0}^{\prime})=\mathrm{res}_{0}\left[\frac{(1+x+b_{0}^{\prime})^{1-\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega+\mathsf{L}}-1}{1-\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega+\mathsf{L}}\times\left(\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\left(\frac{\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}+1}{\mathsf{L}^{2k+1}}\right)\omega^{2k}-1\right)(\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega)d\omega\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>res</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝖫</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝖫</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}(x+b_{0}^{\prime})=\mathrm{res}_{0}\left[\frac{(1+x+b_{0}^{\prime})^{1-\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega+\mathsf{L}}-1}{1-\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega+\mathsf{L}}\times\left(\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\left(\frac{\mathsf{S}_{2k+2}+1}{\mathsf{L}^{2k+1}}\right)\omega^{2k}-1\right)(\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega)d\omega\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">In the middle parenthesized term, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied by <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{10}/\mathsf{L}^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{10}/\mathsf{L}^{11}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we need to take the residue at <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficient of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>
appears when the other terms combine to give <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{11}/\omega^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{11}/\omega^{11}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To get <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{11}/\omega^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{11}/\omega^{11}</annotation></semantics></math> we must use the <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\mathsf{L}/\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mathsf{L}/\omega</annotation></semantics></math> piece of the <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega-\mathsf{L}/\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> term. Similarly, when we expand the first term, only the powers of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}/\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}/\omega</annotation></semantics></math> are relevant. From this, we see that the coefficient of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> in the above display is the negative of the
coefficient of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> in</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{(1+x+b_{0}^{\prime})^{1+y}}{1+y}=\frac{e^{x(1+y)}}{1+y}+\langle\mathsf{L}\rangle=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}(y+1)^{n-1}+\langle\mathsf{L}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝖫</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝖫</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{(1+x+b_{0}^{\prime})^{1+y}}{1+y}=\frac{e^{x(1+y)}}{1+y}+\langle\mathsf{L}\rangle=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}(y+1)^{n-1}+\langle\mathsf{L}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">In conclusion, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n-1\choose 10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n-1\choose 10}</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Getzler’s formulas also encode the <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We recover this information in a different way, by describing generators on which the <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action is evident, as follows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Generators and their pullbacks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To begin, in the case <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Dimension and Hodge structure ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> tells us</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\otimes\mathbb{C}=H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\oplus H^{0,11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\otimes\mathbb{C}=H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\oplus H^{0,11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The weight <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> cusp form of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a distinguished generator <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, p. 14]</cite> for an explicit geometric construction.
It is evident from this construction (or from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>) that <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by the sign representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe a natural collection of forms in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which we will soon see are generators. These forms come from pulling back the distinguished generator of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> under the various forgetful maps <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Precisely, given an ordered subset <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|=11</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{A}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A}\cong\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{A}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A}\cong\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> for the projection map and define <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{A}:=f_{A}^{*}\omega\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{A}:=f_{A}^{*}\omega\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The pullbacks of these forms to boundary divisors follow a simple rule. By the Künneth formula, the only boundary divisors of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with non-zero <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}</annotation></semantics></math> are those of the form</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{B}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,B^{c}\cup q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{B}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,B^{c}\cup q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B|\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B|\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{B}\colon D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{B}\colon D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the map that glues <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, projection onto the first factor <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pr}_{1}\colon D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pr}_{1}\colon D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{*}\colon H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{11}(D_{B})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{*}\colon H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{11}(D_{B}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Given ordered subsets <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|=11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cap B|=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cap B|=10</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a unique element <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\notin B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\notin B</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(A)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ordered set obtained from <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cap B|=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cap B|=11</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is already contained in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, then we set <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(A)=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(A)=A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a boundary divisor <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{B}\colon D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{B}\colon D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|=11</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{B}^{*}\omega_{A}=\begin{cases}\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{*}\omega_{\epsilon(A)}&amp;\text{if $|A\cap B|\geq 10$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{B}^{*}\omega_{A}=\begin{cases}\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{*}\omega_{\epsilon(A)}&amp;\text{if $|A\cap B|\geq 10$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">First suppose <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cap B|\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cap B|\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B^{c}\cap A|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B^{c}\cap A|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a commutative diagram</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.E4.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="72.3" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-35.78px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 254.15 72.3" width="254.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,72.3) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(127.08,0) translate(0,35.78)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -127.08 -25.8)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 71.57)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.52)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 59.83 0) translate(23.56,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -17.6 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.54em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="21.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E4.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces D_{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces D_{B}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 169.43 0) translate(57.63,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -18.47 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.67em;--ltx-fo-height:0.57em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.86)" width="23.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E4.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_M