ath" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 61.37)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(32,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -26.04 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.76em;--ltx-fo-height:0.57em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.86)" width="38.25"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E5.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 64 0) translate(60.3,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -21.13 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.05em;--ltx-fo-height:0.57em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.86)" width="28.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E5.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A},}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A},}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -19.86 24.14 L 23.35 24.14"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 23.63 24.14)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -4.12 28.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.89em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.56" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="12.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E5.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\iota_{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ι</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\iota_{B}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -43.69 13.26 L -43.69 -12.42"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -43.69 -12.7)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -62.78 -1)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.14em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="15.84"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E5.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{pr}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">pr</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{pr}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 48.61 11.38 L 48.61 -12.42"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 48.61 -12.7)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 51.86 -3.22)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.04em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="14.34"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E5.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -11.41 -22.71 L 20.69 -22.71"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.96 -22.71)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -2.26 -32.7)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.04em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="14.34"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E5.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where if <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cap B|=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cap B|=10</annotation></semantics></math>,
we identify <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with the unique symbol of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> not contained in <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Relations and the <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The group <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the subsets <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and correspondingly on the subspace of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that, for any permutation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the subgroup of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> fixing <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\sigma(A)}=\sign(\sigma)\omega_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\sigma(A)}=\sign(\sigma)\omega_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To identify our representation, we briefly recall some of the combinatorial objects that arise in the representation theory of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tabloid</em> is an equivalence class of tableaux, which identifies tableaux up to reordering rows. Given a tableau <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the corresponding tabloid. Given a partition <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> the vector space with basis given by tabloids of shape <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <em id="S2.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Specht module generator</em> associated to a tableau <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\sigma\in\mathrm{C}_{T}}\sign(\sigma)\{\sigma(T)\}\in M_{\lambda}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\sigma\in\mathrm{C}_{T}}\sign(\sigma)\{\sigma(T)\}\in M_{\lambda},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{C}_{T}\subset\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{C}_{T}\subset\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subgroup that preserves the columns of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> setwise.
The subspace of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the vectors (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>) as <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> runs over all tableaux is an irreducible representation called the <em id="S2.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Specht module</em> <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Specht module generators associated to the standard tableaux on <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis for <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To each ordered subset <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>, we associate a tableau <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of shape <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-10,1^{10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-10,1^{10})</annotation></semantics></math> which has the symbols of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in order down the first column and the rest of the first row filled in increasing order.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant isomorphism <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\xrightarrow{\sim}V_{n-10,1^{10}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\xrightarrow{\sim}V_{n-10,1^{10}}</annotation></semantics></math> taking <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> to the Specht module generator associated to <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The dimension of the Specht module <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-10,1^{10}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-10,1^{10}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of standard tableaux of shape <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-10,1^{10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-10,1^{10})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n-1\choose 10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n-1\choose 10}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Dimension and Hodge structure ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, it will suffice to give a <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map from the subspace of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\omega_{A}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{A}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-10,1^{10}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-10,1^{10}}</annotation></semantics></math> that takes <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> to the Specht module generator associated to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We are going to study the image of subspace generated by <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\omega_{A}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{A}\}</annotation></semantics></math> under the pullback map</span></p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\to\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11,0}(D_{B})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\to\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11,0}(D_{B}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is an ordered subset of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is as in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Generators and their pullbacks ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>).
Let <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be the element of the right hand side of (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>) which has component <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{*}\omega\in H^{11,0}(D_{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{*}\omega\in H^{11,0}(D_{B})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in all other components.
The collection of ordered subsets <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> is in bijection with tabloids on <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-10,1^{10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-10,1^{10})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> fills the row and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (in order) fills the column. Thus, the right-hand side is identified with the vector space of tabloids <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n-10,1^{10}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n-10,1^{10}}</annotation></semantics></math>;
given a tabloid <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T\}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> we write <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\{T\}}=W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\{T\}}=W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|=11</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset A</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B|=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B|=10</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\cup B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\cup B</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\notin B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\notin B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let
<math id="S2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{A\to B}\colon i\cup B\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{A\to B}\colon i\cup B\to A</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding permutation.
By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Generators and their pullbacks ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, the image of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> in the right hand side of (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>) is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{A}\mapsto\sum_{B\subset A}\sign(\sigma_{B\to A})W_{B}=\sum_{\sigma\in S_{A}}\sign(\sigma)W_{\{\sigma(T_{A})\}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{A}\mapsto\sum_{B\subset A}\sign(\sigma_{B\to A})W_{B}=\sum_{\sigma\in S_{A}}\sign(\sigma)W_{\{\sigma(T_{A})\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is the Specht module generator defined in (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>), and the proposition follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The forms <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\omega_{A}:1\in A,\text{ $A$ increasing}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> </mtext><mi>A</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> increasing</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{A}:1\in A,\text{ $A$ increasing}\}</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis for <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pullback map <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\to\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11,0}(D_{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})\to\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11,0}(D_{B})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dan Petersen suggested an alternate method to obtain several of the results in this section, which avoids the manipulations with generating functions in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Dimension and Hodge structure ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. We sketch his argument here. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{1}:\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{1}:\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the universal elliptic curve and <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fiber product of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> with itself over <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open substack of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the long exact sequences for the pairs <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n},\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n},\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E}^{n-1},\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E}^{n-1},\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that there are natural isomorphisms</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}^{n-1})\cong W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\cong H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}^{n-1})\cong W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\cong H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">One can then study the Leray spectral sequence for the smooth morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{n-1}:\mathcal{E}^{n-1}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{n-1}:\mathcal{E}^{n-1}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the local system <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}f^{1}_{*}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}f^{1}_{*}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by the Künneth formula,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf^{n-1}_{*}\mathbb{Q}\cong(Rf^{1}_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n-1}\cong(\mathbb{Q}\oplus\mathbb{V}[-1]\oplus\mathbb{Q}(-1)[-2])^{\otimes n-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf^{n-1}_{*}\mathbb{Q}\cong(Rf^{1}_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n-1}\cong(\mathbb{Q}\oplus\mathbb{V}[-1]\oplus\mathbb{Q}(-1)[-2])^{\otimes n-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The pure cohomology <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}^{n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}^{n-1})</annotation></semantics></math> arises from the <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n-1}{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n-1}{10}</annotation></semantics></math> summands <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}[-1]^{\otimes 10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}[-1]^{\otimes 10}</annotation></semantics></math>, each of which gives a copy of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Dimension and Hodge structure ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. To identify the <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> representation as in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 2.3. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, one first notes that as an <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> representation, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}_{n-1})\cong\mathsf{S}_{12}\otimes\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{10}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-11}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(\mathrm{sgn}\boxtimes\mathrm{triv})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>Ind</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊠</mo><mi>triv</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}_{n-1})\cong\mathsf{S}_{12}\otimes\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{10}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-11}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(\mathrm{sgn}\boxtimes\mathrm{triv}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.4" class="ltx_p">By the Pieri formula and the branching rule for the symmetric group, it follows that as an <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> reprsentation,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}_{n-1})\cong\mathsf{S}_{12}\otimes V_{n-10,1^{10}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{E}_{n-1})\cong\mathsf{S}_{12}\otimes V_{n-10,1^{10}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by recalling the inductive method of Arbarello and Cornalba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, by excision of the boundary and pullback to its normalization. We then apply this method to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and a preliminary proposition about the degree <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> cohomology of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Restricting to boundary divisors</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the excision long exact sequence associated to the boundary <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdots\rightarrow H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k+1}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow\cdots." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdots\rightarrow H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k+1}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow\cdots.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that this sequence is in fact a long exact sequence of mixed Hodge structures or <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations. In particular, when <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an injective morphism</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{k}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{k}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the normalization of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Arbarello and Cornalba improve on the injectivity of (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1. Restricting to boundary divisors ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 2.6 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the pullback</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For fixed <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the following proposition gives vanishing of compactly supported cohomology in all but an explicit finite collection of cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proposition 2.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}&amp;k&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1\\
&amp;k&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}&amp;k&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1\\
&amp;k&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>The case of even degrees <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> be the tautological cohomology ring. Tautological classes are algebraic and defined over <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, so if <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate. The next lemma provides the necessary base cases for the inductive argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> is even, then <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, all cohomology is tautological <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, as is all <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">even</em> cohomology for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;20</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Finally, for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, all cohomology is tautological for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We induct on <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.2. The case of even degrees 
          
            
              ≤
              k
              12 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we can assume <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Proposition 2.1 of []). ‣ 3.1. Restricting to boundary divisors ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we have an injection</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Each component of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient by a finite group of
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}+g_{2}=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}+g_{2}=g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+n_{2}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+n_{2}=n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g_{i}-2+n_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g_{i}-2+n_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. By induction on <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we know <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate.
Meanwhile, note that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and odd <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq k/2\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq k/2\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Hence, the Künneth formula shows that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1})=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{k/2}H^{2i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1})\otimes H^{k-2i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1})=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{k/2}H^{2i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1})\otimes H^{k-2i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Inductively, we know that the right hand side of (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 3.2. The case of even degrees 
          
            
              ≤
              k
              12 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) is pure Hodge–Tate. Thus, (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 3.2. The case of even degrees 
          
            
              ≤
              k
              12 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) gives an injection of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> into a Hodge structure or Galois representation that is pure Hodge–Tate, and it follows that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate as well.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>The case of degree <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The base cases required to run an analogous induction for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math> are those where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> does not inject into <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. Recent results of the first two authors rule out any such bases cases with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;11</annotation></semantics></math>, this follows by combining Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Lemma 2.6 of []). ‣ 3.1. Restricting to boundary divisors ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Proposition 2.1 of []). ‣ 3.1. Restricting to boundary divisors ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
If <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> shows <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an injection</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The result is known for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so we may assume <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.3. The case of degree 
          
            
              =
              k
              11 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we have an injection</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Each component of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient by a finite group of
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}+k_{2}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}+k_{2}=n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g_{i}-2+n_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g_{i}-2+n_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,3,5,7,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,3,5,7,9</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, the Künneth formula shows that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Note that either <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}&lt;g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}&lt;g</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}=g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+1&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+1&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, and analogously for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{2},n_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{2},n_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, there is an injective morphism of Hodge structures from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> into a direct sum of Hodge structures of the form <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&lt;g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&lt;g</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>. By double induction on <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and using that <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that there is an injective morphism of Hodge structures or <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}).\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}).\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>An induction via the weight spectral sequence</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove the vanishing of <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
We do so by identifying the injection <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.3. The case of degree 
          
            
              =
              k
              11 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) as the first map in a complex and showing
that the next map in the complex is injective.
The complex we use is obtained from the <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page in the weight spectral sequence associated to the compactification <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, via Poincaré duality. It is also the weight 11 summand of the Feynman transform of the modular operad <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and therefore has a natural graph complex interpretation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, Section 2.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>The first two maps in the weight <math id="S4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> complex</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> denote a stable <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-marked graph of genus <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>; the underlying graph is connected, each vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is labeled by an integer <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the valence of the vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. The stability condition is that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g_{v}+n_{v}-2&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g_{v}+n_{v}-2&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\prod_{v}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\prod_{v}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is the natural gluing map</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The normalization of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}/\Aut(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}/\Aut(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the first map in the weight <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> complex, the pullback of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to the normalization of the boundary, can be rewritten as:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\xrightarrow{\ \alpha\ }\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut\Gamma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛼</mo></mover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\xrightarrow{\ \alpha\ }\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut\Gamma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The target of the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th map in the weight <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> complex is <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=j}(H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut\Gamma}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=j}(H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut\Gamma}.</annotation></semantics></math> Here, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the determinant of the permutation representation of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> acting on the set of edges. We will only need the first and second maps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us describe the second map. We define</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)=1|}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{\ \beta\ }\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)=2|}(H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut(\Gamma)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munder><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛽</mo></mover><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)=1|}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{\ \beta\ }\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)=2|}(H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut(\Gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">as follows.
For each graph <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with two edges, choose an ordering of
the edges <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\Gamma)=\{e_{1},e_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Gamma)=\{e_{1},e_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and say <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the node obtained by gluing the marked points <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the graph with one edge obtained by contracting <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Gluing <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\phi_{i}(\Gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\phi_{i}(\Gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn gives a map <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}^{*}\colon H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\phi_{i}(\Gamma)})\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}^{*}\colon H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\phi_{i}(\Gamma)})\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>. This induces</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\phi_{i}(\Gamma)})\xrightarrow{\xi_{i}^{*}}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ξ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msubsup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\phi_{i}(\Gamma)})\xrightarrow{\xi_{i}^{*}}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-trivial representation of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{2}=\xi_{1}\circ\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{2}=\xi_{1}\circ\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the automorphism that simultaneously swaps <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponding to the automorphism of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> that swaps the two edges). In particular, it follows that the image of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}^{*}-\xi_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}^{*}-\xi_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the subspace <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\subset H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\subset H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by taking the sum over all <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs. Then <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\circ\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\circ\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, because each component is the difference of the pullbacks under two copies of the same gluing maps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, assuming it holds for <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first give a short inductive proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, assuming that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.3. The case of degree 
          
            
              =
              k
              11 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, the map <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1. The first two maps in the weight 
          
            k
          
         complex ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) is injective. We claim that the map
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.1. The first two maps in the weight 
          
            k
          
         complex ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) is also injective. The theorem follows from this claim, since <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\circ\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\circ\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the domain of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)=1|}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)=1|}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has a single vertex with a loop edge, then <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which vanishes by induction on <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has two vertices joined by an edge. Then <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the Künneth formula and the vanishing of lower degree odd cohomology,</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq b</annotation></semantics></math>. By induction on <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes unless <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case it is <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that, in this case, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph obtained by attaching a loop to the vertex of genus <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1</annotation></semantics></math> (and decreasing its genus accordingly), as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2. Proof of Theorem , assuming it holds for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="/html/2209.03113/assets/Gamma2Gamma2p.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="399" height="106" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The graph <math id="S4.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on the left and <math id="S4.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on the right.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As in (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.2. Proof of Theorem , assuming it holds for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>), we know that <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})</annotation></semantics></math> as a summand. Then <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> injectively into this summand of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and it follows that <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, as claimed. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> follows a similar strategy to the inductive argument for <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.3. The case of degree 
          
            
              =
              k
              11 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we know that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, and we claim that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is also injective. The theorem follows from this claim. However, the proof that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is injective is more involved in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by describing some of the components of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> as concretely as possible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the leftmost graph in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3.2. Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
In this case, <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> are not isomorphic, so each component of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta|_{H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta|_{H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})}</annotation></semantics></math> is one of the usual restriction maps, up to sign.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the rightmost graph in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3.2. Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
In this case, <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(\Gamma)\cong\phi_{2}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(\Gamma)\cong\phi_{2}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> be the automorphism that simultaneously swaps <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponding to the automorphism of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> swapping the two edges). Then <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{2}=\xi_{1}\circ\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{2}=\xi_{1}\circ\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 2.3. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, we know <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}\colon H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\to H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}\colon H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\to H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplication by <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta|_{H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta|_{H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})}</annotation></semantics></math> is again one of the usual restriction maps, up to rescaling.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will show that, for each <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graph <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> there is a collection of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Gamma_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Gamma_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math> injectively into <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i}(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{i}})\otimes\det(E_{\Gamma_{i}}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i}(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{i}})\otimes\det(E_{\Gamma_{i}}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we order the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs in such a way that, at each step, none of the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Gamma_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Gamma_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> admit edge contractions to any of the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs that came earlier in the order. In this way, we see that <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by a block upper diagonal matrix with injective blocks, and hence is injective, as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomology <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and hence <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, as required. The cases <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> can be handled by an argument similar to that used for the cases <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math>, below, but the details are more involved. Instead, we note that <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is rational in these cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and hence, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.3. The case of degree 
          
            
              =
              k
              11 ‣ 3. Applying the Arbarello–Cornalba induction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, it follows that <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. For the remainder of this subsection, we therefore assume <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">There are three types of <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs to consider: those with a vertex of genus <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, those with a unique vertex of genus <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and a self-edge, and those with two vertices of genus <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Graphs with a genus <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertex</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with one edge and a genus <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertex. Then <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cup p|&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cup p|&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,A\cup p})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,A\cup p})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by induction. By the Künneth formula and the vanishing of lower degree odd cohomology, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2. </span>Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph with one vertex of genus <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> legs, and one self-edge.
Consider <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> labeled <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n,p_{1},q_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n,p_{1},q_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are glued together to get curves with dual graph <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
As in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Generators and their pullbacks ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, given a subset <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\{1,\ldots,n,p_{1},q_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\{1,\ldots,n,p_{1},q_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of cardinality <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{B}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p_{2}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,B^{c}\cup q_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{B}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p_{2}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,B^{c}\cup q_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. (This is nonempty because <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math>.)
There are four flavors of subsets <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>:
both of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>; neither <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>; only <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>; or only <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
These correspond to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> types of codimension <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> strata under the map <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> that glues <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.3.2. Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="/html/2209.03113/assets/Divisors1.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="573" height="99" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>The four flavors of <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each subset <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be the two-edge graph obtained by further gluing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3.2. Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Note that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}}=D_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}}=D_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to show that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{|B|=10}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}}\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{B})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{|B|=10}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}}\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{B})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is injective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When we glue <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the last two types of subsets <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> give the same type of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graph <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="/html/2209.03113/assets/Divisors2.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="573" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Gluing <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Swapping <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the first two maps <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{B}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> but exchanges the other two. Correspondingly, the edge representation is trivial in the first two cases and non-trivial in cases three and four.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Corollary 2.5. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we have an injection</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(D_{B})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(D_{B}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Now, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on both sides by swapping <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariants of both sides, we have an injection from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> into</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}\{p_{1},q_{1}\}\subset B\text{ or }\{p_{1},q_{1}\}\subset B^{c}\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(D_{B})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\oplus\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}p_{1}\in B\text{ and }q_{1}\notin B\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}[H^{11}(D_{B})\oplus H^{11}(D_{(B\smallsetminus p_{1})\cup q_{1}})]^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}\{p_{1},q_{1}\}\subset B\text{ or }\{p_{1},q_{1}\}\subset B^{c}\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(D_{B})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\oplus\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}p_{1}\in B\text{ and }q_{1}\notin B\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}[H^{11}(D_{B})\oplus H^{11}(D_{(B\smallsetminus p_{1})\cup q_{1}})]^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">The first collection of terms is <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the graphs <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of the first and second flavor in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3.2. Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which have trivial edge representation.
For the terms in square brackets, we have <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(D_{B})\cong H^{11}(D_{(B\smallsetminus p_{1})\cup q_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(D_{B})\cong H^{11}(D_{(B\smallsetminus p_{1})\cup q_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-action switches the two factors. Hence, the space of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariants is <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\in B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}\notin B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∉</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}\notin B</annotation></semantics></math>, note that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is the type of graph considered in Example <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Example 4.2. ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. In particular <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\Gamma_{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\Gamma_{B})</annotation></semantics></math> acts by the sign representation, so
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{B})}=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{B})}=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In summary, we have given an injection</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{|B|=10}(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{B})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{|B|=10}(H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{B}})\otimes\det E(\Gamma_{B}))^{\Aut(\Gamma_{B})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3. </span>Graphs with two genus <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has two genus <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, so</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We may assume <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|\leq|A|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|\leq|A|</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.3. Graphs with two genus 
            
              1
            
           vertices ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows three types of graphs that will be used in the three cases of the argument. Note that none of them have a loop, so none admit edge contractions to the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs of Section <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS2" title="4.3.2. Graphs with a single genus 1 vertex ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="/html/2209.03113/assets/phipsigraphs.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="638" height="94" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The graphs in Cases 1, 2, and 3.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case 1: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> By Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Corollary 2.5. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, there is an injection</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}B\subset A\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}B\subset A\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first graph in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.3. Graphs with two genus 
            
              1
            
           vertices ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, which has</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,(A\smallsetminus B)\cup\{q^{\prime},p\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,\{q\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,(A\smallsetminus B)\cup\{q^{\prime},p\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,\{q\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case 2: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Again, by Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Corollary 2.5. ‣ 2.3. Relations and the 
          
            
              
              S
              n
            
          
        -action ‣ 2. Genus 
        
          1 ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, there is an injection</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}B\subset A\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}^{\prime}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\hookrightarrow\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}B\subset A\\
|B|=10\end{subarray}}H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}})=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}^{\prime}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where this time <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the second graph in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.3. Graphs with two genus 
            
              1
            
           vertices ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, which has</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}^{\prime}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,(A\smallsetminus B)\cup\{q^{\prime},p\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Psi_{B}^{\prime}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,B\cup p^{\prime}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,(A\smallsetminus B)\cup\{q^{\prime},p\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Note that our assumptions <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|=1</annotation></semantics></math> ensure <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\smallsetminus B|\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\smallsetminus B|\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so the middle genus <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> vertex has at least <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> markings. Note that no edge contraction of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graph <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of the type in Case 1 or a different <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of the type in Case 2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case 3: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Choose any <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in A^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in A^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,\ell\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,\ell\in A</annotation></semantics></math> and define</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A^{c}\cup q}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A^{c}\smallsetminus\{i,j\})\cup\{q,p^{\prime}\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,\{q^{\prime},i,j\}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad X_{A\cup p}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A\smallsetminus\{k,\ell\})\cup\{p,p^{\prime}\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,\{q^{\prime},k,\ell\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A^{c}\cup q}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A^{c}\smallsetminus\{i,j\})\cup\{q,p^{\prime}\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,\{q^{\prime},i,j\}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad X_{A\cup p}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A\smallsetminus\{k,\ell\})\cup\{p,p^{\prime}\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,\{q^{\prime},k,\ell\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Associated to these are <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graphs <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> so that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times X_{A^{c}\cup q}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A^{c}}}=X_{A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times X_{A^{c}\cup q}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A^{c}}}=X_{A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and
contracting the edge connecting <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. See the last graph in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.3. Graphs with two genus 
            
              1
            
           vertices ‣ 4.3. Proof of Theorem  for 
          
            
              =
              g
              2 ‣ 4. An induction via the weight spectral sequence ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for a picture of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that no edge contraction of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graph appearing in Cases 1 or 2 above or a different <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge graph of the type in Case 3.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By the Künneth formula,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A^{c}\smallsetminus\{i,j\})\cup\{q,p^{\prime}\}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A^{c}\smallsetminus\{i,j\})\cup\{q,p^{\prime}\}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody><tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="5">and</td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A^{c}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A^{c}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A\smallsetminus\{k,\ell\})\cup\{p,p^{\prime}\}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,(A\smallsetminus\{k,\ell\})\cup\{p,p^{\prime}\}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">It follows that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> injects into the sum</p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A}})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A^{c}}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A}})\oplus H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Phi_{A^{c}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It should also be possible to deduce the vanishing of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> from the work of Dan Petersen as follows. There are exact sequences</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})(-1)\rightarrow H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{2,n})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+2})(-1)\rightarrow H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{2,n})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Specializing to <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\cong W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{2,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\cong W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{2,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Theorem 2.1 and Remark 2.2]</cite>, there is an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{2,n})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=11}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\mathrm{ct}},A^{q})\oplus H^{p}(\Sym^{2}\mathcal{M}_{1,1},B^{q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{2,n})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=11}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\mathrm{ct}},A^{q})\oplus H^{p}(\Sym^{2}\mathcal{M}_{1,1},B^{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are certain direct sums of Tate twists of symplectic local systems. The cohomology of these local systems has been determined by Petersen in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Petersen’s work makes significant use of high-powered machinery, including mixed Hodge modules, perverse sheaves, the decomposition theorem for the map <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\mathrm{ct}}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\mathrm{ct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ct</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\mathrm{ct}}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\mathrm{ct}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Eichler–Shimura isomorphism concerning modular forms. The proof we present here is relatively elementary and highlights a combinatorial perspective on the vanishing of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Application to point counting</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The weighted count of <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> points on a Deligne–Mumford stack <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q}):=\sum_{x\in|X(\mathbb{F}_{q})|}\frac{1}{\#\Aut(x)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q}):=\sum_{x\in|X(\mathbb{F}_{q})|}\frac{1}{\#\Aut(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X(\mathbb{F}_{q})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X(\mathbb{F}_{q})|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of isomorphism classes of the groupoid <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>. This point count is related to the trace of the Frobenius map <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> on cohomology by Behrend’s Grothendieck–Lefschetz trace formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 3.1.2]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q^{\dim X}\sum_{k\geq 0}(-1)^{k}\tr\Phi_{q}|H^{k}_{\text{\'{e}t}}(X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mtext>ét</mtext><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q^{\dim X}\sum_{k\geq 0}(-1)^{k}\tr\Phi_{q}|H^{k}_{\text{\'{e}t}}(X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Using this formula in the case <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to the proof of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Corollary 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Corollary 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}=3g-3+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}=3g-3+n</annotation></semantics></math>.
The eigenvalues of Frobenius acting on <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{\text{\'{e}t}}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mtext>ét</mtext><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{\text{\'{e}t}}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> are Weil numbers of weight <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that under any embedding <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_{\ell}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> they have absolute value <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{k/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{k/2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Thus, the point count <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is determined up to <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(q^{d-13/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(q^{d-13/2})</annotation></semantics></math> by the eigenvalues of Frobenius on <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and Poincaré duality tells us that Frobenius acts on the even cohomology <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{d-k/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{d-k/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the groups <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanish for odd <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Thus, up to <math id="S5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(q^{d-13/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(q^{d-13/2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the only other contribution to <math id="S5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is from <math id="S5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d-11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d-11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which vanishes when <math id="S5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and Poincaré duality.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Higher odd cohomology groups</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>. The main tool is the push-pull formula. This formula is proven for manifolds in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Corollary 2.2]</cite>. The proof for orbifolds or smooth Deligne–Mumford stacks goes through analogously.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon X\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon X\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed embedding of codimension <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> between smooth Deligne–Mumford stacks. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{X/Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{X/Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the normal bundle. Then for any cohomology class <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{*}i_{*}(\alpha)=c_{d}(N_{X/Y})\cup\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{*}i_{*}(\alpha)=c_{d}(N_{X/Y})\cup\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1+\sum_{i=1}^{k}g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1+\sum_{i=1}^{k}g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is ordered so that <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}&lt;\dots&lt;g_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}&lt;\dots&lt;g_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It suffices to prove the result for <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math>, as the pull back maps</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">are injective for all <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the gluing morphism</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},1}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{k},1}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},1}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{k},1}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">attaching the marked point on the <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{i},1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{i},1}</annotation></semantics></math> component to the <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th marked point on <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonzero holomorphic <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>-form. We will show that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{*}(\alpha\otimes 1\otimes\dots\otimes 1)\neq 0\in H^{11+2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{*}(\alpha\otimes 1\otimes\dots\otimes 1)\neq 0\in H^{11+2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to show that <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi^{*}\xi_{*}(\alpha\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{*}\xi_{*}(\alpha\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero. If <math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, set</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U:=\mathcal{M}_{1,11}\times\mathcal{M}_{g_{1},1}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{M}_{g_{k},1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U:=\mathcal{M}_{1,11}\times\mathcal{M}_{g_{1},1}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{M}_{g_{k},1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, set</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U:=\mathcal{M}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}\times\mathcal{M}_{g_{2},1}\times\cdots\mathcal{M}_{g_{k},1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U:=\mathcal{M}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}\times\mathcal{M}_{g_{2},1}\times\cdots\mathcal{M}_{g_{k},1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text">We will show that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi^{*}\xi_{*}(\alpha\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)|_{U}\neq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{*}\xi_{*}(\alpha\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)|_{U}\neq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.7.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g,11-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the stable graph with <math id="S6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices as follows. One vertex is of genus <math id="S6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11-k</annotation></semantics></math> half-edges, and the rest of the vertices are of genus <math id="S6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is exactly one edge between the first vertex and each of the latter vertices, and no other edges, unless <math id="S6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> in which case we allow ourselves to replace the <math id="S6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> vertex with a genus <math id="S6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> vertex with a self-edge (see Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 6. Higher odd cohomology groups ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="/html/2209.03113/assets/graphpossibilities.png" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="449" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>The graphs <math id="S6.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g,11-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g,11-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the open substack of <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing curves whose dual graphs are obtained from <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g,11-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math> by edge contraction.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi|_{U}\colon U\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi|_{U}\colon U\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">factors through the open substack <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g,11-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The induced map</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon U\hookrightarrow V_{g,11-k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon U\hookrightarrow V_{g,11-k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">is a closed embedding between smooth stacks of codimension <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we are using that the genera <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\dots,g_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\dots,g_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct. Let <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> denote the restriction of <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is nonzero (for example, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>). By Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 6.1. ‣ 6. Higher odd cohomology groups ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a></span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i^{*}i_{*}(\beta\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)=c_{k}(N_{i})\cup(\beta\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{*}i_{*}(\beta\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)=c_{k}(N_{i})\cup(\beta\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{g_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{g_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the (unique) <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> class on the <math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j+1)</annotation></semantics></math>st component of <math id="S6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S6.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th <math id="S6.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> class on <math id="S6.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is well-known that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(N_{i})=\prod_{j=1}^{k}(-\psi_{j}\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1-1\otimes\cdots\otimes\psi_{g_{j}}\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(N_{i})=\prod_{j=1}^{k}(-\psi_{j}\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1-1\otimes\cdots\otimes\psi_{g_{j}}\otimes\cdots\otimes 1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">See, for example, the discussion in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Section A.4]</cite>.
Because <math id="S6.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\mathcal{M}_{1,11})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\mathcal{M}_{1,11})=0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, all products of the form <math id="S6.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish. Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(N_{i})\cup(\beta\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)=(-1)^{k}(\beta\otimes\psi_{g_{1}}\otimes\cdots\otimes\psi_{g_{k}})\neq 0.\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(N_{i})\cup(\beta\otimes 1\otimes\cdots\otimes 1)=(-1)^{k}(\beta\otimes\psi_{g_{1}}\otimes\cdots\otimes\psi_{g_{k}})\neq 0.\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Application to Chow rings</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> the Chow ring of <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Kimura <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, Totaro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>)<span id="S7.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth, proper Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a countable <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space, then the cycle class map</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)\rightarrow H^{*}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)\rightarrow H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, note that <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over a subfield <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that is finitely generated over <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence countable. Suppose <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is countable. Then there is a countable extension <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X_{F})\rightarrow A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X_{F})\rightarrow A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. Let <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finitely generated extension of <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be embedded in <math id="S7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we have a morphism</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X_{F})\rightarrow A^{*}(X_{G})\rightarrow A^{*}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X_{F})\rightarrow A^{*}(X_{G})\rightarrow A^{*}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Each of the maps above is injective by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Proposition 3.2]</cite>.
Because the composite is surjective, it follows that the first morphism is also surjective. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite>, it follows that the motive of <math id="S7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate, and thus the motive of <math id="S7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is as well. Note that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite> is stated for schemes, but the same proof goes through for Deligne–Mumford stacks. In particular, the cycle class map is an isomorphism.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The tautological ring <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite dimensional <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Corollary 1]</cite>. By Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1 (Kimura [], Totaro []). ‣ 7. Application to Chow rings ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, we know that <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is uncountable whenever there is odd cohomology. Therefore, the quotient <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})/R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})/R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is uncountable for the values of <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The eleventh cohomology group of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>. It follows that the subgroup of <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by cycles algebraically equivalent to zero is also uncountable, since the Hilbert scheme has only countably many connected components.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
